{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And he offered sacrifices to the Elohim of his father Isaac.</b> A person is obligated in the honor of his father more than in the honor of his grandfather; therefore he attached it to Isaac and not to Abraham — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 46:1). But this is not sufficient, for the fitting thing would be to say \"to the Elohim of his fathers\" without singling out one person, as he said, \"the Elohim before whom my fathers walked, Abraham and Isaac\" (Genesis 48:15), and in his prayer he said, \"Elohim of my father Abraham and Elohim of my father Isaac\" (Genesis 32:10), or he should say \"and he offered sacrifices to YHWH,\" as it says regarding Abraham (Genesis 12:7), \"and he built an altar to YHWH\" — and what need is there to elaborate further? But this verse contains a sod; they will reveal it to us there in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 94:5), for when Jacob came to go down to Egypt he saw that the exile would begin with him and with his seed, and he feared it, and he offered many sacrifices for the fear of his father Isaac, so that the attribute of judgment not be drawn out against him. And he did this at Be'er-sheva, which is a house of prayer for his fathers, and from there he took permission when he went to Haran. And Scripture says \"sacrifices\" (זְבָחִים), to inform us that they were not burnt-offerings like those of his fathers, for Abraham brought burnt-offerings, and our Sages said (Zevachim 116a) that the children of Noah did not offer shelamim (peace-offerings); they brought burnt-offerings, and regarding Noah it is written explicitly (Genesis 8:20), \"and he offered burnt-offerings on the altar.\" But Jacob, out of the fear of YHWH, brought shelamim, to reconcile all the attributes to Himself, as they expounded (Torat Kohanim, Leviticus 16:1) — shelamim (peace-offerings): they impose peace in the world. Now behold, the beginning of his intention was in the attribute of Gevurah (Might), which is close to him, and this is the reason they mentioned in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 94:5) that a person is obligated in the honor of his father more than in the honor of his grandfather, and it is the reason they said there in another version: at first one inquires after the welfare of the disciple and afterward one inquires after the honor of the master.\n\nAnd I saw in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKana (Sefer HaBahir 135) in this language: \"And Jacob swore by the fear of his father Isaac\" (Genesis 31:53) — is there a person who swears thus, by the fear of his father? Rather, the power had not yet been given to Jacob, and he swore by the power that had been given to his father, as it says, \"And Jacob swore by the fear of his father Isaac.\" And what is it? It is what is written of it (I Kings 18:38), \"And the fire of YHWH fell and consumed the burnt-offering,\" and it is written (Deuteronomy 4:24), \"For YHWH your Elohim is a consuming fire,\" and so on — thus far in the midrash. And from their words we learn that because of this he did not say \"and he offered sacrifices to YHWH,\" for by now Jacob had already merited his portion, as it says (Micah 7:20), \"You give truth to Jacob, lovingkindness to Abraham, which You swore to our fathers from days of old.\" And it was necessary to explain now — and behold, by the merit of the offerings, the Elohim of his father Isaac appeared to him in visions of the night with the attribute of judgment softened; this is what it says, \"Elohim in visions of the night,\" and it is what he said, \"I am El, the Elohim of your father,\" for He is the El of Beit El who said to him in Haran, \"I am the El of Beit El, where you anointed a pillar\" (Genesis 31:13), and He is \"the Elohim of your father\" — He is the Name, and it is the attribute. And He promised him that he should not fear in Egypt, for he will merit in his judgment and will be redeemed after the affliction, and this is the reason for \"and I will surely bring you up.\"\n\nAnd the Rav wrote in chapter 27 in the first part of the Guide of the Perplexed, that the translation of \"I will go down with you to Egypt, and I will surely bring you up\" — \"אֲנָא אֵיחוֹת עִמָּךְ וַאֲנָא אַסְּקִנָּךְ\" (I will escort you and I will bring you up), and the Rav was astonished by this according to the opinion of Onkelos. And he said that Onkelos exerted all his effort to remove corporeality from every narrative in the Torah, and whenever he finds these names indicating a species of movement, he assigns the concept of movement to the account of a created being or to providence from the El, and translates \"and YHWH descended\" (Exodus 19:20) as \"and YHWH was revealed,\" \"I will go down now and see\" (Genesis 18:21) as \"I will be revealed now and see\" — and if so, why did he translate here \"אֲנָא אֵיחוֹת\" (I will escort)? And the Rav explained that because at the beginning of the matter it says, \"And Elohim spoke to Israel in visions of the night,\" it would not have been difficult for Onkelos to narrate the utterance as it was said in visions of the night, for it is a narrative of speech and not a narrative of an actual deed that took place. For there is a great difference between what is said in a dream or a vision of the night and what is said in a vision and an appearance, and what is said in a decisive utterance, \"And the word of YHWH came to me, saying,\" or \"And YHWH said to me, saying\" — these are his words. And thus he said (the Rav in Guide I:47) that Onkelos removed hearing everywhere, and assigns its interpretation as the reaching of that matter to the Creator or acceptance of prayer, and translates \"hearing before YHWH\" (Onkelos on Genesis 29:33) and \"I will surely accept his offering\" (Exodus 22:22). And if the matter is as the Rav said, why should Onkelos flee from movement and remove hearing also out of fear that it teaches corporeality, yet not flee from saying and speaking, nor from calling, in any place — whether in a dream, in a vision, or in a decisive utterance, for in all of them he translates \"and YHWH said,\" \"and YHWH spoke,\" \"and YHWH called to Moshe\" — and that too teaches corporeality? And he should have translated \"and it was said before YHWH\" or \"the word of YHWH was\" or \"YHWH was proclaimed,\" as fitting for the matter, as the Rav explained regarding speech and saying (Guide I:64). And why should he remove hearing but not remove seeing, which he translates \"and YHWH saw\"? And what the Rav said (ibid. 47), that it indicates the concept of intellectual apprehension just as it indicates visual apprehension — all the more so is this the case with hearing, for it is said of intellectual apprehension and of will in many places, such as \"And Abram listened to the voice of Sarai\" (Genesis 16:2), \"Hear the voice of my supplications\" (Psalms 28:2), \"Even when you multiply prayer, I do not hear\" (Isaiah 1:15), \"Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice\" (I Samuel 15:22), \"And it shall be if you indeed obey the voice of YHWH your Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 28:1), and similarly \"a hearing heart\" (I Kings 3:9), and similarly most of them. Now behold, Onkelos should not have feared hearing, for it teaches nothing but acceptance of the matter and will toward it, and he did not flee from seeing in any place, but translated it according to its plain meaning everywhere, that the matter is apprehended by seeing alone. But when it is not apprehended by seeing alone and requires attention and contemplation, he translates as fitting for it, as when he said \"for YHWH has seen my affliction\" (Genesis 29:32), \"I have surely seen the affliction of My people\" (Exodus 3:7), \"and Elohim saw the children of Israel\" (Exodus 2:25) — that the seeing regarding them is not that He sees their bodies, but that He attends to their circumstances and knows them. And this is his way throughout the Torah, not as the reason that occurred to the Rav, which became confused in his hand.\n\nAnd the other two places that he translated (Genesis 6:12, 29:31) \"וַחֲזָא אֵלָיו\" — he did not complete them (consistently). And in the language of \"passing\" Onkelos used an expression and translated \"and YHWH passed before him\" (Exodus 34:6) as \"and the Shekhinah of YHWH passed before him,\" that the passing thing should be a created being by His will, and he does not mention a term of movement in the Creator, as the Rav mentioned. And if so, how did he translate \"YHWH your Elohim, He passes before you\" (Deuteronomy 31:3) — \"YHWH your Elohim, He has passed before you\" — and behold this is a kind of movement in the narrative of a deed, and Onkelos was not afraid of it! And likewise in the verse \"And Israel saw the great hand\" (Exodus 14:31), he translated it \"and Israel saw the great might of the hand,\" dropping the word \"גְּבוּרַת\" (might) because of the expression \"which He did,\" yet he left in it \"the great hand\" and was not afraid and fearful of the hand, and did not characterize it at all. And so he conducted himself in the translation of \"written by the finger of Elohim\" (Exodus 31:18) — \"by the finger of YHWH.\" And what the Rav explained regarding it (Guide I:65), that Onkelos assigns the finger as a created instrument through which the tablets were opened by the will of the Creator, is not true, for behold, \"from His right hand, a fiery law for them\" (Deuteronomy 33:2) — \"from His right hand He wrote,\" and he did not fear the writing right hand, which teaches corporeality, and similarly the finger. And he further translated (Exodus 15:12) \"You stretched out Your right hand,\" and said (ibid. v. 6), \"Your right hand, O YHWH, shatters the enemy\"; and translated \"Your mighty hand\" (Deuteronomy 3:24) — \"Your mighty hand\"; and said (ibid. 4:34) \"with a mighty hand and with an uplifted arm\"; and similarly \"and it seizes hold in judgment, My hand\" (Deuteronomy 32:41) — \"and is mighty in judgment, My hand\"; and so he translated (ibid. 11:12) \"the eyes of YHWH your Elohim are always upon it.\" And behold, regarding Jacob it says at the beginning of the matter, \"And he dreamed, and behold, a ladder set up on the earth\" (Genesis 28:12), and Onkelos feared it and translated \"and behold, the splendor of YHWH stood upon it,\" and did not translate \"and behold YHWH,\" because it is in a dream. And he translated \"and behold, I am with you\" (ibid. v. 15) — \"and behold, My Memra supports you\" — and did not say \"and behold, I am with you,\" as he translated \"I will escort with you,\" even though it is a narrative of a thing said in a dream, equal to this one. And he translated \"and I will be with your mouth\" (Exodus 4:12) — \"and I will be with your mouth,\" and said regarding \"For I will be with you, and this is the sign for you\" (ibid. 3:12) — \"for My Memra will be with you.\"\n\nAnd furthermore Onkelos was careful in dreams and translated \"And Elohim came to Avimelech in a dream of the night\" (Genesis 20:3) and \"And Elohim came to Lavan in a dream\" (Genesis 31:24) — \"And there came a Memra from before YHWH.\" And if you say that he was concerned lest the coming precede the dream, and one might think it is literal — behold, regarding Solomon it is written (I Kings 3:5), \"In Givon YHWH appeared to Solomon in a dream of the night,\" and Yonatan translated it, \"YHWH was revealed to Solomon.\" And if the matter was said in a dream, they would narrate it as it was said in that dream, and let it not be difficult for them, even though it is an utterance that teaches corporeality, for its being in a dream resolves it for them that it is not literal. Moreover, the fact that he says it was seen in a dream makes it fitting that they narrate it as it is, for what is said about it — that it is a dream of the night — teaches that it is not literal, but a dream that appeared to him as such. And do not think that this was because Yonatan ben Uziel could not express the language of seeing regarding dreams in Aramaic, for \"and I saw in my dream\" (Genesis 41:22) is translated \"וַחֲזֵית,\" and regarding Nebuchadnezzar \"חָזֵה הֲוֵית\" (Daniel 2:31). And so Onkelos translated \"Your complaints are not against us but against YHWH\" (Exodus 16:8) — \"against the Memra of YHWH\" — and there is no fear or dread of corporeality here. And similarly \"And the people spoke against Elohim and against Moshe\" (Numbers 21:5) — \"And the people became embittered against the Memra of YHWH\"; and similarly \"between me and you\" (Genesis 9:12), \"between Elohim and every living soul\" (ibid. v. 16) — \"between My Memra\" and \"the Memra of YHWH,\" and many similar to these. And similarly he translated \"May YHWH watch\" (Genesis 31:49) — \"May the Memra of YHWH watch\"; \"Elohim is witness\" (ibid. v. 50) — \"The Memra of YHWH is witness\" — and he has no fear of corporeality in them, and also \"Memra\" has no relevant meaning here, that one should say of it that it watches and testifies. And similarly \"Swear to me by Elohim\" (ibid. 21:23) — \"Confirm to me by the Memra of YHWH\" — and those who swear do not mention, \"I swear by the utterance of Elohim,\" and similar to these are many for Onkelos, and their sod is known to the enlightened.\n\nAnd similarly regarding the language of standing, the Rav said (Guide I:27) that Yonatan ben Uziel intended in it to explain permanence, and therefore translated \"and His feet will stand\" (Zechariah 14:4) as \"and He will be revealed in His might,\" and similarly translated every matter of motion and activity as \"might.\" Now behold, Onkelos was not afraid of the language of standing, and translated \"Behold, I stand before you there, on the rock\" (Exodus 17:6) — \"Behold, I am standing before you there on the rock.\" And what the Rav said (ibid.), that Onkelos assigns the concept of movement to the revelation of the Shekhinah and to appearances of created glory — behold, Onkelos would flee from glory more than from giving it these expressions, and would translate \"and the glory of YHWH appeared to all the congregation\" (Numbers 16:19) — \"and the glory of YHWH was revealed,\" as he says \"and YHWH was revealed\" (Exodus 19:20), and would not translate \"and the glory of YHWH was seen.\" And so he translates regarding angels (Exodus 3:2) — \"was revealed\" — and if Onkelos spoke of angels and created glory in matters of corporeality as the Rav said (Guide I:26), it would have been fitting that he not fear seeing that a person sees, and translate in them \"was seen,\" as he did: \"For I have seen an angel of Elohim face to face\" (Genesis 32:31) — \"for I have seen an angel of YHWH.\" And far be it, heaven forbid, that the thing called Shekhinah or glory should be a created being separate from the glorious Name, blessed be He, as the Rav thought here and in many chapters of his book. For he translated \"If Your presence does not go\" (Exodus 33:15) — \"If Your Shekhinah does not journey among us\" — and Moshe would not desire a created glory separate from the glorious Name, blessed be He, to go with him, for the Holy One, blessed be He, had already said to him, \"Behold, My angel will go before you\" (Exodus 32:34), and he was not satisfied with that, but sought that the El Himself go with him in His glory (ibid. 33:14). And so after he heard HaShem's voice and He said to him, \"Also this thing that you have spoken, I will do\" (ibid. v. 17), Moshe said, \"Let Adonai go now in our midst\" (ibid. 34:9) — \"Let the Shekhinah of YHWH now go in our midst.\" And so he translated \"You cannot see My face\" (ibid. 33:20) — \"You cannot see the face of My Shekhinah, for no man can see Me.\" And Yonatan ben Uziel said (Ezekiel 3:12) — \"Blessed be the glory of YHWH from the place of the abode of His Shekhinah.\" And if this glory — if the verse means by it the Creator Himself and His truth, and it is like \"Show me now Your glory\" (Exodus 33:18), which the Rav explained thus (Guide I:53, 63) — behold, he mentioned in it \"place\" and \"abode of His Shekhinah.\" And if one says it is a created glory, according to the Rav's opinion regarding the verse \"and the glory of YHWH filled the tabernacle\" (Exodus 40:35) and the like — how can they establish in it \"Blessed\"? And one who blesses and prays to a created glory is like an idol worshipper. And in the words of our Sages, many things teach that the name Shekhinah is the El, blessed be He.\n\nBut these matters — for Onkelos and Yonatan ben Uziel they are well-known things in the Kabbalah, and their sod is for those who know grace. And at the stand of Mount Sinai, Onkelos translates every occurrence of Elohim mentioned in the passage as \"glory\" or \"Memra of YHWH,\" and when the passage mentions the unique Name, he does not say so — and all with providence and wisdom from Him. And I will further mention this, with the help of the Name, blessed be He (Exodus 20:19). And what Onkelos translated, \"And Elohim spoke all these words\" (Exodus 20:1) — \"and YHWH spoke\" — his reason for this is because it says, \"Face to face YHWH spoke to your entire congregation\" (Deuteronomy 5:4, 19), and the enlightened will understand. But what he says here, \"I will escort with you,\" he intended to allude to what they said (Mekhilta de-Shirata 3): They were exiled to Egypt — the Shekhinah was with them, as it says, \"I will go down with you to Egypt\"; they were exiled to Elam — the Shekhinah was with them, as it says (Jeremiah 49:38), \"And I will set My throne in Elam.\" Now behold, the saying and the descending are equivalent, as I explained above, and he could not translate otherwise in any manner, as I alluded (at the beginning of this passage). But there regarding Jacob it was not fitting to translate \"and behold, I am with you,\" because there it is written, \"and behold, YHWH stood upon it\" (Genesis 28:13), and the enlightened will understand. And because Onkelos found that it is not as its plain meaning literally, he fled from it and made it merely the concept of help, and said \"My Memra supports you,\" and did not say \"My Memra is with you,\" as he said regarding Moshe (Exodus 3:12). And may the El show us wonders from His Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {0 "This is a very long single comment containing multiple theological arguments. The entire Hebrew text is one continuous comment block on Genesis 46:1; I have preserved paragraph breaks at natural argument transitions for readability while translating the whole faithfully."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:23:46.546777796Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}